  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Morning fog gives way to a mainly dry and \nseasonable Sunday. \n\nEarly this morning, high pressure extends from the Great Lakes to \nthe Northeast and is nudging south into the local area. A \ndissipating stationary boundary is located near or just south of the \nVA/NC border. North of the front some residual moisture remains in \nplace with dew points in the upper 60s to low 70s. With the residual \nmoisture and calm winds, areas of fog and low stratus are possible \nthis morning. Interior portions of the area and especially the VA \nPiedmont have the best chances of seeing fog this morning. Any fog \nquickly dissipates shortly after sunrise.\n\nFor this afternoon, high pressure builds over the local area leading \nto a seasonable and mainly dry day. A stray shower or storm cannot \nbe ruled out across far southern portions of the area due to the \nlingering boundary, but overall chances are low (PoPs <20%). High \ntemperatures today will be around or slightly above average for late \nAugust, with highs expected to range from the mid 80s to around 90. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of the week with increasing chances for showers and storms \nby late week.\n\nA strong 500mb upper level ridge expands eastward over the Ohio \nValley and into the Mid-Atlantic early this week. Warm advection and \ndeep mixing will allow for temperatures to rise into the low to mid \n90s on Monday and Tuesday (locally upper 90s on Tuesday). Max heat \nindices on Monday and Tuesday should generally average 100 to 104 \ndue to deep mixing keeping dewpoints in the upper 60s to around 70. \nSubsidence under the the upper ridge will cap most convective \ndevelopment, limiting any shower/storm chances to <15% (a very \nisolated shower/storm due to the afternoon sea breeze is possible). \n\nThe ridge peaks in strength Wednesday before flattening later in the \nweek. Wednesday will see high temperatures likely in the mid to \nupper 90s for many inland locations. The NBM even shows a few 100 \ndegree readings across southwest portions of the area, but this is \nlikely too warm and is a known bias. Still, Heat Advisories may be \nneeded for at least southern and southeastern portions of the area \non Wednesday, especially if dewpoints overachieve. The ridge begins \nto break down on Thursday as an upper level trough moves across the \nGreat Lakes and pushes an advancing cold front toward the Mid-\nAtlantic region. Thursday may be the hottest day of the period due \nto heating ahead of the front and a slight increase in humidity. \nHighs on Thursday will range from the mid to upper 90s for most \ninland locations. Heat Advisories may be needed for much of the area \non Thursday. Conditions likely become more unsettled later Thursday \nand especially on Friday into the weekend as the front slowly pushes \ninto the local area. AI/machine learning tools continue to hint at a \nsevere weather potential on Thursday and Friday, but this will \nultimately depend on the timing of the front.",
